<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The rights the *bundled interpreter* is signed with.

  Entitlements go on executables. `python3.12` is the process that opens the
  microphone, so `python3.12` is what carries the right; the sentence the user
  reads lives in the app's own Info.plist, and the TCC probe confirmed it is that
  string, beside the app's name, that appears — `python3.12` never does.

  Two things about this file are deliberate and easy to misread later:

  * It is **the only extra entitlement in v0.** No `allow-jit`, which is added
    only if the CFFI audio callback actually crashes (the escape hatch is
    blocking-mode PortAudio), and no `disable-library-validation` at all.
  * Without the App Sandbox or the hardened runtime — neither of which v0 turns
    on, because v0 is not notarized — this entitlement is **inert**, belt and
    braces rather than load-bearing. Bundle containment is what earns the grant
    (ADR 0005). It is here so the bundle is already the right shape when the
    notarization-era ticket turns the hardened runtime on, which is also where
    `allow-jit` becomes live again.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
